# PoliLingo — environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values. `.env.local` is gitignored and must
# never be committed.
#
#   Copy-Item .env.example .env.local
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE THAT MATTERS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# In Next.js, any variable named NEXT_PUBLIC_* is compiled into the JavaScript bundle
# and downloaded by every visitor. It is published, permanently, to anyone who opens
# the site. Everything without that prefix is readable only by server-side code.
#
# Getting this wrong with the Supabase service-role key would hand every visitor a
# credential that bypasses Row Level Security on the entire database. The `Secrets`
# CI workflow fails the build if a variable name combines NEXT_PUBLIC_ with
# SERVICE_ROLE, SECRET, PRIVATE_KEY, PASSWORD or _TOKEN.
#
# When you add a variable anywhere, add it here in the same pull request. This file is
# the authoritative list of what the app expects. See docs/configuration.md.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — accounts, the workspace and live content (docs/platform.md 4.1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The app needs exactly two Supabase variables, and both are public by design.
# Leave them empty and the build still passes, learner pages work exactly as
# they always have (static, progress in this browser), and the workspace
# (/review, /edit, /admin) says it is not configured.
#
# There is no service-role or secret key in this app, here or in Vercel, and
# there never will be. Every privileged action is a database function that
# checks auth.uid(), and every table has Row Level Security.
#
# Local development: start the shared local stack (npm run db:start), then
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=<the local anon/publishable key from
#                                          npx supabase status -o env>

# Supabase project URL. Safe to publish.
# Dashboard -> Project Settings -> Data API -> Project URL
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The publishable key (sb_publishable_…; the legacy anon key also works).
# Public BY DESIGN: it identifies the project and authorises nothing on its
# own. Its safety rests entirely on Row Level Security and on the database
# functions checking who is calling.
# Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API Keys -> Publishable key
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D5 — native audio library (arrives ~week 5)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Public base URL for lesson audio, served from the polilingo-audio-pub R2 bucket
# through its custom domain. Safe to publish; it is in every <audio> element anyway.
NEXT_PUBLIC_AUDIO_BASE_URL=

# R2 credentials are NOT set here. The app reads public audio over plain HTTPS and
# needs no S3 credentials at runtime. Writing to R2 happens in the content repository's
# release workflow. If you ever find yourself adding an R2 secret to this app, stop and
# ask why the app is writing to object storage.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Content
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Content needs no variable. content/release.json is the baseline learner copy
# every build ships with. Newer releases are published in the app (/admin/publish)
# and reach browsers at run time through /api/release, using the two Supabase
# variables above. Settings shows which release a learner is using.
#
# There is no content channel either. A learner copy holds only lessons whose
# publish gate is open, so there is nothing gated for a variable to reveal.
# Gated content (Hindko until it is reviewed) is seen only in the workspace.
#
# VERCEL_ENV is set by Vercel, not here. When it is `production`, next.config.ts
# refuses a content/release.json that is not a tagged release (content@YYYY.MM.N).


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D10 — analytics (arrives ~week 8)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# There is no third-party analytics key here and there should never be one. ADR-0015
# records the decision to use first-party analytics only: practice events go into our
# own Postgres, with no tag manager, no ad SDK and no third-party script on the learner
# path. If someone proposes adding one, that is an ADR, not an environment variable.
